Suppose a root-only reader with any finite installed state set succeeds
on every actual origin with the same code and at most two calls.
All four displayed origins start at its one state $\iota(0)$, since
their complete code is zero and all other entry fields are common.
An initial Halt would return one fixed whole row and cannot be correct
on these four different targets. Thus the initial state is a Read.

Every possible first representative root has a reply shared by at least
three of these four origins. Indeed, $a$ and $c$ separate $v_3$ from
$\{v_0,v_1,v_2\}$; $b_1$ isolates $v_1$, $b_2$ isolates $v_2$,
$b_3$ isolates $v_3$, and $b_4$ is constant. After taking that common
first reply, at least three origins with three different required rows
are in exactly the same successor state. At most one invocation remains.

If this state is a Halt, it returns just one row. If it is a Read, its
fixed root address has at most two different actual replies on the four
origins, hence at most two on the selected three. For each such reply
there is one fixed successor. Every attained successor must be a Halt:
a running successor would have to make a third native call, and the
contract supplies no free transition or output decoder. Therefore this
second Read can return at most two whole rows. Neither possibility
serves the three different targets, a contradiction.

This argument ranges over the competing table itself. It assumes no
relationship to the address paths or state paths of Theorem 4.1.
Any amount of state sharing or duplication, either choice of the other
code's entry, early halts, repeated roots, or cycles in the union graph
is covered by the same common-state argument. Unattained transition
entries add no actual replies. The all-occurrence matrix duplicates the
partner columns and gives the identical obstruction when either partner
occurrence may be read. The four witnesses refute a universal correctness
promise; they do not restrict the domain of the upper construction.
The exclusion thus has no installed-state upper bound. $\square$

